  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 100 AM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nToday: Foggy Start Then Mostly Dry\n\nExpecting a foggy morning across much of the region with \npockets of dense fog observed along and north of I-94 where \nskies have cleared earlier overnight as compared to those \nfurther south. As skies continue to clear north to south over\nthe next few hours, areas towards I-90 may see dense fog \ndevelop, especially across southeast Minnesota where 28.00z \nREFS/HREF probabilities for 1/4mi visibility or less sit around \n30-60%. Regardless, fog and reduced visibilities may make \ndriving difficult for the Monday morning commute, so make sure \nto slow down and give yourself some extra time when heading out \nthe door! Fog should linger for a few hours after sunrise, \ndissipating between 9-10am. After the fog dissipates, \npredominantly dry conditions along with partly to mostly cloudy \nskies are expected through the rest of the day.\n\nTuesday - Thursday: Widespread Rainfall Potential\n\nA longwave trough moves into the western United States Tuesday,\nallowing for a cutoff low to develop over the Desert Southwest \nwhile a northern stream shortwave continues eastward into the \nUpper Midwest. Ahead of this feature, a ribbon of positive \nvorticity extends from the Southwest into the Midwest atop \ntropospheric deep southerly flow ushering a healthy plume of \nmoisture (PWATs of 1.7-1.9 inches) northward which combined with\nforcing associated with the approaching shortwave should result\nin a period of widespread rainfall Tuesday afternoon through \nThursday.\n\nWhile the atmosphere appears primed for a period of heavy \nrainfall, there is still uncertainty with regard to where the \ngreatest rainfall amounts end up, especially given a northward \ntrend of higher rainfall amounts in the past 24 hours. 27.12z \nLREF cluster analysis suggests there are two primary scenarios. \nThe first being a more northern solution, largely comprised of \nGEFS members, associated with a slower trough progression and \nfurther northward transport of deep moisture which would suggest\nrainfall would be favored along and north of I-90. The second \nscenario, largely composed of EPS members, suggests the trough \nmoves through a bit quicker and rainfall remains primarily along\nand south of I-90. These scenarios are also noted in the AI \ncounterparts to these traditional NWP models.\n\nRegardless of which scenario wins out, it appears evident that \nthe southerly flow and consistent moisture transport northward \ncombined with the synoptic scale forcings sets up an extended \nperiod of rainfall across the region. Current 48-hour QPF \nprobabilities in the 28.01z NBM through Thursday morning suggest\namounts of 1.5-2 inches are favored and currently have a 50-70%\nchance. However, there is some probability for amounts of 3-4 \ninches across the region, currently around 20-30% in the 28.01z\nNBM. There is some concern for areal and river flooding with \nthis event, especially if the southern scenario plays out given \nhigher residual moisture content from the rainfall a little over\na week ago south of I-90. \n\nFriday into the Weekend: Drier and Trending Cooler\n\nThe plume of deep moisture should get pushed off to the south \nThursday into Friday as troughing attempts to dig south over the\nGreat Lakes into the northeastern US. There is quite a large \ndiscrepancy on just how far south and how quickly the trough \ndigs south out of Canada, which may have a pretty large impact \non the weather going into next weekend. This feature will usher \nin a cold front, which will lead to cooler temperatures, just \nhow cool is the main question. As of now, afternoon highs are \nforecast to be in the mid to lower 60s but overnight lows may \ndrop into the lower 40s to 30s if the cooler solutions pan out.",
